a court in hong kong has convicted seven  prominent pro-democracy activists and  politicians for their role in  anti-government protests in 2019.  two other defendants had previously  pleaded guilty now these defendants they  included the man known as hong kong's  father of democracy  martin lee he is the 82-year-old founder  of the democratic party  also 73-year-old media tycoon jimmy ly  he's one of the most outspoken critics  of the current administration  and margaret ung a 73 year old former  lawmaker  nearly every leading voice of opposition  in hong kong is now  in jail on trial or in exile dw's phoebe  kong met up with activist lee check  young  before the verdict for a look at the  decades-long  democracy struggle against beijing  a small flat in downtown hong kong where  the world's only museum dedicated to the  1989  tiananmen crackdown is located the  military repression in beijing  32 years ago changed the course of many  people in china  and hong kong including lee check in  i was there during the june 4 massacre  and i was detained for three days  after my release back to hong kong i  pledged that i would spend my lifetime  in fighting for the democracy in china  and so that those who sacrifice  will not be in vain decades on  lee che gen is facing a different fate  critics see another massive crackdown  being carried out by beijing  this time on its most liberal and  democratic city  hong kong in the wake of anti-government  protest  he and eight others were charged for  organizing and attending an unauthorized  assembly  that rally in which over a million  people took part  was one of the few without violent  clashes  every one of the defendants are profile  figures  apart from media tycoon jimmy lai or are  former lawmakers or icons of the  opposition movement  like martin lee who is known as father  of democracy  each of the charges carries a maximum 5  year jail term  this case is just a tip of the iceberg  with over 10 thousand others arrested  for lee choi yang is the first verdict  among the nine  charges for which he's on trial  beijing's recently imposed sweeping  national security law  and his overhaul of the city's electoral  system intensified the fear  leech yen organized hong kong's cinnamon  morning vigil every year  calling for an end to china's one-party  rule for decades  an act that could make him a prime  target and beijing's security law  this year he will likely be absent for  the very first time  in this era in a way that going to jail  is part of the struggle now  i would comfort myself by saying that  this is already  something of a blessing for me that i  would only have to go to jail after you  know 40 years of activism  in hong kong and but i'm ready to pay  the price and ready to face it  having witnessed hong kong's  decades-long struggle the 64-year old  activist  is not optimistic for he believes that  his successes will continue the fight  for democracy  until they finally reached a goal  and for more on this let's bring in  nathan law pro-democracy activist from  hong kong and former student leader  joining us from london where he  is in exile nathan thanks for joining us  on the program today uh you know those  who have been convicted  they are veterans of the pro-democracy  movement what is your reaction to these  verdicts and  what do you think it means for them  um so this case is just one of  the case in such a massive suppression  um we've got  we've already got 47 uh prominent  experts being arrested under the  national security law  and um these seven defendants they're  all my  friends and um great athletes  contributed a lot on hong kong's  democratic movement and um this is  definitely a political persecution that  they are being completed because of  peace of assemblies  okay so you call it a a a political  persecution as you mentioned this is  just  one of uh or rather a few of several  cases that are before the court system  um i'd like to ask you about the others  that are as well for example the 47  pro-democracy politicians and activists  who have been charged with subversion  under the new security law  for participating in an election primary  that  prosecutors say was part of a plan to  subvert the government  do you think they will also be convicted  well we have to understand the nature of  the national security court  the government can pack their judge  there is  no jury available and the government  could choose to  conduct the hearing without any presence  of the public  so if you look into the condition of  that  and you look into the ordinance of the  national security law which is written  really quickly  and that is literally a large room for  interpretation  by the judge and by the courts so i  think uh the possibility of them being  convicted  is actually quite high so  i think this is another example of how  the government uses  the national security law to push some  dissident voice and the world should  continue to pay attention to it  given that nathan put it into the big  picture for us  what does it mean for the pro-democracy  movement what does it mean for the legal  system  so for now the the protein muscle c  movement  has stepped into a low types it's like  we're stepping into a winter storm  that every one of us has to face it and  um possibly the people tends to light  low  but if someone is still resisting they  will receive  serious repercussion but i don't think  that will  kill all the determination of us  fighting for democracy  i think many of us have already uh  been jailed and see it as uh one  of the cost that we have to pay on the  role of struggle  so i think many of them actually have  the mental  preparation for that and for the rule of  law in hong kong it's all gone  under the national security law and  people's freedom of expression and  freedom of association  are being all annihilated so i think  that is a sad  reality of hong kong and nathan these  seven convictions that we've seen today  they come amid efforts by china to only  allow quote patriots to be part of the  legislature  you know beijing and these pro-beijing  lawmakers  for their part they say that the  pro-democracy camp has been disruptive  on the streets disruptive in the  legislature  where things could no longer get done  how do you respond to that  the pursuit for democracy is completely  legitimate um  in 1997 when hong kong was handed back  from the british government to the  chinese government  the chinese government promised hong  kong people  that we will gradually get our democracy  and it should have happened  um five to ten years ago so for now  i think um beijing has always been  trying to  use these excuse to conceal the fact  that they're breaking promise breaking  international treaty  and they are depriving hong kong people  from their legitimate rights  and they're using legal persecution to  silence us  once again so um i think it is important  that  the world understands the direction of  the protest behind hong kong people  and also um well seeing these  all these persecution as another wave of  human resolution from china and try to  hold them accountable  thank you so much for joining us nathan  law pro-democracy activist from hong  kong former student leader  on this day where as we have mentioned a  court in hong kong  has convicted seven prominent  pro-democrat democracy activists nathan  thank you for sharing your view thank  you  you
 

